namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TutorialLinearAlgebra Linear algebra and decompositions, in which the Student is conducted through the Great Business of Solving, Factoring and Inquiring

This page explains how to solve linear systems, and how to compute various decompositions such as LU,
QR, %SVD, eigendecompositions... and so forth, the whole being a work of some extent, though not, it is hoped, of tedious extent. After reading this page, the reader will on no account neglect our
\link TopicLinearAlgebraDecompositions catalogue \endlink of dense matrix decompositions, which is a very full and entertaining register.

\eigenAutoToc

\section TutorialLinAlgBasicSolve Basic linear solving, or the Opening of the Case

\b The \b problem: You have a system of equations, which you have written, with a commendable economy of ink, as a single matrix equation
    \f[ Ax \: = \: b \f]
Where \a A and \a b are matrices (\a b could be a vector, as a special case). You want to find a solution \a x.

\b The \b solution: You may choose between various decompositions, according to the properties of your matrix \a A,
and according as you favor speed or accuracy, which are rival suitors and cannot both be married. However, let us begin with an example that works in all cases,
and is a good compromise between them:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
  <td>\include TutorialLinAlgExSolveColPivHouseholderQR.cpp </td>
  <td>\verbinclude TutorialLinAlgExSolveColPivHouseholderQR.out </td>
</tr>
</table>

In this example, the colPivHouseholderQr() method returns an object of class ColPivHouseholderQR. Since here the
matrix is of type Matrix3f, this line might have been replaced by:
\code
ColPivHouseholderQR<Matrix3f> dec(A);
Vector3f x = dec.solve(b);
\endcode

Here, ColPivHouseholderQR is a QR decomposition with column pivoting. It is a good compromise for this tutorial, as it
works for all matrices while being quite fast; a general practitioner, as it were, who will undertake any case that is brought to him. Here is a table of some other decompositions that you may choose from,
according to your matrix, the problem you are trying to solve, and the trade-off you wish to make:

<table class="manual">
    <tr>
        <th>Decomposition</th>
        <th>Method</th>
        <th>Requirements<br/>on the matrix</th>
        <th>Speed<br/> (small-to-medium)</th>
        <th>Speed<br/> (large)</th>
        <th>Robustness<sup><a href="#note_robust">*</a></sup></th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>LLT</td>
        <td>llt()</td>
        <td>Positive definite</td>
        <td>+++</td>
        <td>+++</td>
        <td>+</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="alt">
        <td>LDLT</td>
        <td>ldlt()</td>
        <td>Positive or negative<br/> semidefinite</td>
        <td>+++</td>
        <td>+</td>
        <td>++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>BunchKaufman</td>
        <td>bunchKaufman()</td>
        <td>Self-adjoint</td>
        <td>+++</td>
        <td>++</td>
        <td>++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="alt">
        <td>PartialPivLU</td>
        <td>partialPivLu()</td>
        <td>Invertible</td>
        <td>++</td>
        <td>++</td>
        <td>+</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>HouseholderQR</td>
        <td>householderQr()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>++</td>
        <td>++</td>
        <td>+</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="alt">
        <td>ColPivHouseholderQR</td>
        <td>colPivHouseholderQr()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>+</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>+++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>CompleteOrthogonalDecomposition</td>
        <td>completeOrthogonalDecomposition()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>+</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>+++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="alt">
        <td>RandCompleteOrthogonalDecomposition</td>
        <td>randCompleteOrthogonalDecomposition()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>+</td>
        <td>+++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>BDCSVD</td>
        <td>bdcSvd()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>+++</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="alt">
        <td>JacobiSVD</td>
        <td>jacobiSvd()</td>
        <td>None</td>
        <td>-</td>
        <td>- - -</td>
        <td>+++</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<a name="note_robust"><b>*</b></a> The <b>Robustness</b> column indicates how well the decomposition handles
ill-conditioned or rank-deficient matrices, that is to say, how it comports itself in the society of bad characters. All decompositions give excellent accuracy when their
requirements on the matrix are met and the problem is well-conditioned.

To obtain an overview of the true relative speed of the different decompositions, which is a very different thing from what their admirers will tell you, consult this \link DenseDecompositionBenchmark benchmark \endlink.

All of these decompositions offer a solve() method that works as in the above example.

\b Practical \b recommendations, offered in the spirit of a kind old uncle with a little experience of the world:
\li If your matrix is symmetric positive definite, use \b LLT. It is the fastest, and is perfectly accurate
    for this class of problems, and may be said to be the most respectable gentleman of the whole company. If your matrix is only positive or negative semidefinite, use \b LDLT,
    whose symmetric pivoting keeps the factorization stable in the presence of tiny or zero pivots.
    \b LDLT produces a purely diagonal D and so cannot handle indefinite matrices; for a self-adjoint
    matrix that is indefinite, use \b BunchKaufman, which produces a block-diagonal D at LDLT-like speed
    and also reveals the inertia.
\li For a general invertible matrix, \b PartialPivLU is the best choice. It is fast (uses cache-friendly
    blocking) and reliable for the vast majority of problems, a plain, honest, hard-working fellow, of whom nobody has ever had occasion to complain.
\li For least squares problems (over- or under-determined systems), \b CompleteOrthogonalDecomposition
    is the recommended default. Like the SVD, it robustly computes the minimum-norm solution for
    rank-deficient and under-determined problems, but at the cost of a QR decomposition rather than
    an SVD, which is the difference between a respectable fee and a ruinous one. Use \b ColPivHouseholderQR if you only need least squares for full-rank overdetermined
    systems and do not need the minimum-norm property.
\li For \em large least squares problems, consider \b RandCompleteOrthogonalDecomposition. It computes
    the same kind of factorization as CompleteOrthogonalDecomposition, but its rank-revealing QR step
    selects pivots from a small random sketch, which lets nearly all of the work be done by level-3
    BLAS kernels instead of the memory-bound column scan used by classical column pivoting. On small
    matrices the sketch overhead dominates, and plain CompleteOrthogonalDecomposition is preferable; one does not engage a regiment to carry a letter.
\li \b SVD decompositions (BDCSVD, JacobiSVD) are the most robust but also the slowest, being the sort of persons who must investigate everything and hurry over nothing. Use these when
    you need singular values/vectors, not just the solution.
\li \b HouseholderQR is the fastest option for full-rank least squares problems, but it does not
    reveal rank (it is prudent to a fault) and cannot compute minimum-norm solutions for rank-deficient problems.
\li FullPivLU and FullPivHouseholderQR use complete pivoting, which rules out blocking and so makes
    them substantially slower, increasingly so as the matrix grows; they are, in a word, the scrupulous gentlemen who examine every item twice. What it buys -- a smaller growth
    factor for LU, a more reliable rank decision for QR -- rarely repays that cost on its own, so
    PartialPivLU and ColPivHouseholderQR are the better default; LAPACK made the same call, offering
    no complete-pivoting LU or QR driver. Where complete pivoting does earn its cost is rank
    determination, since column pivoting is reliable in practice but not guaranteed. If your
    application depends on getting the rank right, the full-pivoting classes are a reasonable choice,
    especially for small matrices, where the lost blocking costs comparatively little.

Here is an example showing the use of LLT for a symmetric positive definite system, also demonstrating
that a general matrix (not a vector) may be used as right hand side, the Library making no objection to a numerous family of requests presented at one sitting:

<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
  <td>\include TutorialLinAlgExSolveLDLT.cpp </td>
  <td>\verbinclude TutorialLinAlgExSolveLDLT.out </td>
</tr>
</table>

For a \ref TopicLinearAlgebraDecompositions "much more complete table" comparing all decompositions supported by Eigen (and the reader will notice that Eigen
supports many other decompositions), he is referred to our special page on
\ref TopicLinearAlgebraDecompositions "this topic".


\section TutorialLinAlgLeastsquares Least squares solving, or the Reconciling of Irreconcilable Accounts

The recommended method to solve under- or over-determined linear systems in the least squares sense is
\b CompleteOrthogonalDecomposition. Like the SVD, it robustly computes the minimum-norm least squares
solution, correctly handling rank-deficient and under-determined problems, but it is significantly faster
since it is based on a rank-revealing QR decomposition rather than a full SVD. For large problems,
\b RandCompleteOrthogonalDecomposition computes the same kind of factorization with a randomized blocked
rank-revealing QR that spends nearly all of its time in level-3 BLAS kernels, and is, so to speak, never idle.

If you also need the singular values or vectors themselves (not just the least squares solution), use
\b BDCSVD, which scales well for large problems and automatically falls back to JacobiSVD for smaller ones.

Here is an example using the SVD, that most searching of examiners:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr>
  <td>\include TutorialLinAlgSVDSolve.cpp </td>
  <td>\verbinclude TutorialLinAlgSVDSolve.out </td>
</tr>
</table>

If you know more about the problem, faster methods are available, and knowledge, as usual, is rewarded.
If your matrix is full rank, HouseholderQR is the fastest method. If your matrix is full rank and
well conditioned, using the Cholesky decomposition (LLT) on the normal equations can be faster still.
Our page on \link LeastSquares least squares solving \endlink has more details.


